course: dictionary
lesson: 955
title: Blotches and patches
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A blotch is a large, uneven mark or patch of colour. It is not a neat dot or a thin line. It is a big, messy splodge. «The Giraffe was covered all over with chestnut blotches.» The chestnut blotches are patches of brown with edges that are not straight.
explain: Context gives clues. A blotch can be an ink stain on paper, a mistake in a painting, or a red patch on skin. Look at the words around it: uneven, patch, covered all over. Then swap in your meaning and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Blotch can also be a verb, meaning to mark something with blotches. «The Leopard was blotched with spots so he could hide.» If your meaning of "mark with patches" fits the sentence, you have found the verb.
fact: A blotch is a large, uneven mark or patch of colour.
fact: A blotch can be an ink stain, a mistake in a painting, or a red patch on the skin.
fact: In old schools, pupils used blotting paper to soak up ink blotches.
fact: In the Just So Stories, the Leopard gets blotchy and spotty patterns so he can hide in the forest shade.
fact: On skin, red blotches can come from crying, heat, an allergy or an illness. If blotches spread or you feel ill, tell an adult.
exercise: «The Giraffe was covered all over with chestnut blotches.» What is a blotch? | A long, thin straight line | A tall tree in the forest | *A large, uneven patch of colour | A soft, warm breeze
exercise: Which word fits the gap? «The spilled ink left a big, messy ___ on his page.» | stripe | *blotch | whisper | sunbeam
exercise: Which of these is a blotch rather than a stripe? | A thin, straight black line | A narrow band of colour running all the way round | A row of tiny dots in a neat pattern | *A big, uneven red patch on the skin
test: In the verb sense, what does blotch mean? | To pull a tight rope | *To mark something with blotches | To climb very fast | To sing a quiet song
test: Red blotches spread across your skin and you feel ill. What should you do? | Hide them under your jumper | *Tell an adult | Paint over them | Ignore them and hope they go
exam: What does «blotches» mean in «covered all over with chestnut blotches»? | Thin silver lines | Soft feathers | Hot, sunny days | *Large, uneven patches of colour
